## 7. Subnetting Fundamentals

Subnetting divides an IP address space into smaller logical networks.

It allows administrators to organize devices and control communication between different groups.

### 7.1 Understanding CIDR Notation

CIDR stands for Classless Inter-Domain Routing.

Example:

`192.168.10.0/24`

The `/24` prefix means that 24 bits are used for the network prefix.

For a conventional IPv4 `/24` subnet:

- Subnet mask: `255.255.255.0`
- Total addresses: 256
- Normally usable host addresses: 254

Another example:

`192.168.10.0/26`

This has a 26-bit network prefix.

It contains 64 total addresses, normally with 62 usable host addresses in a conventional IPv4 subnet.

### 7.2 Common IPv4 Prefixes

| CIDR | Subnet mask | Total addresses | Normally usable hosts |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| /24 | 255.255.255.0 | 256 | 254 |
| /25 | 255.255.255.128 | 128 | 126 |
| /26 | 255.255.255.192 | 64 | 62 |
| /27 | 255.255.255.224 | 32 | 30 |
| /28 | 255.255.255.240 | 16 | 14 |
| /29 | 255.255.255.248 | 8 | 6 |
| /30 | 255.255.255.252 | 4 | 2 |

The usable-host counts above are for ordinary IPv4 subnets, excluding the network and broadcast addresses.

### 7.3 Example Subnet Design

A segmented lab could use the following networks:

| Network | Subnet | Intended purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Management | 192.168.10.0/24 | Administration of infrastructure |
| Servers | 192.168.20.0/24 | Server workloads |
| Lab | 192.168.30.0/24 | Experimental virtual machines |
| Guest | 192.168.40.0/24 | Guest devices |
| IoT | 192.168.50.0/24 | Smart and network-connected devices |

These are example networks, not confirmed configurations.

### 7.4 Why Subnetting Matters

Subnetting helps:

- Organize devices.
- Reduce unnecessary broadcast traffic.
- Separate different types of equipment.
- Apply routing and firewall policies.
- Make network documentation clearer.
- Scale a network as the lab expands.

A separate subnet does not automatically make devices secure. Routing and firewall rules must enforce the intended separation.

