## 8. VLAN Architecture

A Virtual Local Area Network (VLAN) divides a switched network into separate logical Layer 2 networks.

VLANs help separate devices without requiring a completely separate physical switch for every network.

### 8.1 Example VLAN Plan

| VLAN ID | VLAN name | Example subnet | Purpose |
|---:|---|---|---|
| 10 | MANAGEMENT | 192.168.10.0/24 | Infrastructure administration |
| 20 | SERVERS | 192.168.20.0/24 | Server workloads |
| 30 | LAB | 192.168.30.0/24 | Experimental systems |
| 40 | GUEST | 192.168.40.0/24 | Guest devices |
| 50 | IOT | 192.168.50.0/24 | IoT devices |

### 8.2 Why Use VLANs?

Without VLANs, devices connected to the same unsegmented Layer 2 network may communicate directly, subject to their host firewalls and other controls.

VLANs create separate Layer 2 broadcast domains.

For example:

- Management devices can be separated from experimental virtual machines.
- Guest devices can be isolated from server management.
- IoT equipment can be separated from sensitive systems.
- Server workloads can have more restrictive network policies.

### 8.3 VLAN Diagram

```text
                    ROUTER
                       |
                 VLAN-CAPABLE LINK
                       |
                MANAGED SWITCH
                       |
       +---------------+---------------+
       |               |               |
       |               |               |
    VLAN 10          VLAN 20          VLAN 40
  MANAGEMENT        SERVERS           GUEST
       |               |               |
   Admin PC        Server/VMs       Guest devices
```

The router or another Layer 3 device must provide routing between VLANs when communication is required.

### 8.4 Access Ports

An access port normally carries traffic for one assigned VLAN.

Example:

- A desktop connected to a management VLAN access port.
- A guest device connected to a guest VLAN access port.

The endpoint usually does not need to know the VLAN tag is present on the switch port.

### 8.5 Trunk Ports

A trunk port can carry traffic for multiple VLANs using VLAN tags, commonly IEEE 802.1Q.

Example connections:

- Router to managed switch.
- Managed switch to a virtualization host.
- Managed switch to another managed switch.

The devices at both ends must be configured consistently.

### 8.6 VLAN Requirements

To use VLANs properly, confirm that the equipment supports the required functions:

- VLAN-capable router or Layer 3 device.
- Managed switch with 802.1Q support.
- Compatible server NICs and drivers.
- Operating system or hypervisor VLAN support.
- Firewall policies between networks.
- Correct access and trunk port configurations.

Do not enable VLANs on a live management connection without a recovery plan.

