## 9. Routing

Routing moves IP packets between different IP networks.

A router or Layer 3 device examines the destination IP address and selects an appropriate route.

### 9.1 Default Route

A default route is used when a more specific route is not available.

A home router often provides the path from the private network to the internet.

### 9.2 Inter-VLAN Routing

Inter-VLAN routing allows devices in separate VLANs to communicate through a Layer 3 device.

For example:

```text
VLAN 10: MANAGEMENT
          |
          |
       ROUTER
          |
          |
VLAN 20: SERVERS
```

The router can apply firewall policies to decide whether traffic from management devices is allowed to access server services.

### 9.3 Routing Policy Examples

| Source | Destination | Example policy |
|---|---|---|
| Management | Server network | Allow required administration |
| Management | Lab network | Allow controlled access |
| Guest | Management | Deny |
| Guest | Servers | Deny by default |
| IoT | Management | Deny by default |
| Servers | Internet | Allow required outbound traffic |
| Lab | Management | Deny by default unless needed |

These are starting policy examples, not universal rules. Specific application requirements may justify exceptions.

